
  Next: what "persist across the app" means concretely. This is a storage-format and lifetime decision, and it's the one
  that's awkward to reverse once other forms read from it.

  - [1mIn-memory module[0m — a small [38;5;153msession[39m module holds the current user; everything imports it. Simplest, nothing to clear,
    no storage to go stale. But the id is gone on page reload, so a user who refreshes is anonymous again until they
    log in.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reload, scoped to the one tab, cleared when the tab closes. Matches "logged in until I
    leave" intuitions and needs no logout plumbing for the common case. Serialized, so the shape becomes a small
    contract.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restart and is shared across tabs. This is "stay logged in," which implies you also
    need explicit logout, staleness handling, and a decision about what happens when the stored id no longer matches the
    server. Most capable, most obligations.

  I recommend [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m with the in-memory module in front of it: consumers only ever talk to the module, so
  storage is an implementation detail and moving to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change rather than a hunt through
  every form. The persistence choice only leaks if you let forms touch [38;5;153mStorage[39m directly, which the design won't.

  One caution worth stating now: a user id in browser storage is an identifier, not a credential. It tells you who the
  client [3mclaims[0m to be and nothing more, so nothing security-relevant should trust it — the server still has to authorize
  every request on its own. That's fine for tracking; I just don't want the stored id mistaken for proof of login
  later.
